<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<handout xml:id="day-16-notes">
  <title>Day 16: Sections 1.3, 3.4b, 3.5: Derivatives of Trigonometric Functions and Derivatives as Rates of Change</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      <term>Last Time:</term> We learned about Newton's method, an iterative method used to approximate the roots of functions (or solutions of equations).
    </p>
    <p>
      <term>Today:</term> We want to do a quick recap of trig functions and learn how to differentiate them, and then we'll take a look at derivatives in a bigger context.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <page>
    <paragraphs xml:id="p-trig-derivative-formulas">
      <title>Derivative Formulas of the Day</title>
      <p>
        Here are the derivatives you are expected to know:
        <md>
          <mrow>\frac{d}{dx} \sin(x) \amp = \cos(x), \qquad \frac{d}{dx} \cos(x) = -\sin(x)</mrow>
          <mrow>\frac{d}{dx} \tan(x) \amp = \sec^2(x), \qquad \frac{d}{dx} \cot(x) = -\csc^2(x)</mrow>
          <mrow>\frac{d}{dx} \sec(x) \amp = \sec(x)\tan(x), \qquad \frac{d}{dx} \csc(x) = -\csc(x)\cot(x)</mrow>
        </md>
      </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs xml:id="p-trig-review">
      <title>Trigonometric Review</title>
      <p>
        Before exploring trigonometric derivatives, let's take a moment to review trigonometric functions.
      </p>
      <p>
        Geometrically, we define the trigonometric functions of an acute angle using the side ratios of a right triangle.
      </p>

      <sidebyside widths="38% 58%" valign="top">
        <image source="pictures/sec13pic3.png">
          <shortdescription>
            Right triangle labeled with angle theta, opposite side, adjacent side, and hypotenuse, along with the six primary trigonometric ratios.
          </shortdescription>
        </image>
        <stack>
          <p>
            There are also a few identities that will be helpful to remember:
            <md>
              <mrow>\sin^2(\theta) + \cos^2(\theta) \amp = \underline{\hspace{0.5in}}</mrow>
              <mrow>\tan^2(\theta) + 1 \amp = \underline{\hspace{1in}}</mrow>
              <mrow>\tan(\theta) \amp = \frac{\underline{\hspace{1in}}}{\underline{\hspace{1in}}}, \quad \cot(\theta) = \frac{\underline{\hspace{1in}}}{\underline{\hspace{1in}}}</mrow>
              <mrow>\csc(\theta) \amp = \frac{\underline{\hspace{1in}}}{\underline{\hspace{1in}}}, \quad \sec(\theta) = \frac{\underline{\hspace{1in}}}{\underline{\hspace{1in}}}</mrow>
            </md>
          </p>
        </stack>
      </sidebyside>

      <p>
        The unit circle, special triangles, and the ASTC (<q>All Students Take Calculus</q>) rule shown below can help organize and recall common values of <m>\sin(\theta)</m> and <m>\cos(\theta)</m>. We recommend reviewing these topics. While you are expected to know trigonometric values at key angles, you don't need to memorize the whole unit circle.
      </p>

      <sidebyside widths="32% 32% 30%" margins="2% 2%" valign="top">
        <image source="pictures/sec13pic7.png">
          <shortdescription>Unit circle diagram showing key angles in radians and degrees with their (cos, sin) coordinates.</shortdescription>
        </image>
        <image source="pictures/sec13pic5.png">
          <shortdescription>Special 45-45-90 and 30-60-90 right triangles showing exact side length ratios.</shortdescription>
        </image>
        <image source="pictures/sec13pic6.png">
          <shortdescription>Four-quadrant ASTC chart indicating where All, Sine, Tangent, and Cosine functions are positive.</shortdescription>
        </image>
      </sidebyside>
    </paragraphs>
  </page>

  <page>
    <paragraphs xml:id="p-exploring-sin-cos-derivatives">
      <title>Derivatives of Sine and Cosine</title>
      <p>
        For now, let's move on to differentiating trig functions. We'll start by looking at the graphs of <m>\sin(x)</m> and <m>\cos(x)</m> below and exploring the derivative of the sine function.
      </p>
      <p>
        Recall the relationship between <m>f(x)</m> and <m>f'(x)</m> in the following table:
      </p>

      <tabular top="minor" left="minor" right="minor" bottom="minor">
        <col width="40%" halign="center"/>
        <col width="55%" halign="center"/>
        <row bottom="minor">
          <cell><m>f(x)</m></cell>
          <cell><m>f'(x)</m></cell>
        </row>
        <row bottom="minor">
          <cell>Increasing/Decreasing</cell>
          <cell><fillin characters="12"/> / <fillin characters="12"/></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell>Concave Up/Down</cell>
          <cell><fillin characters="18"/> / <fillin characters="18"/></cell>
        </row>
      </tabular>

      <p>
        Consider <m>\sin(x)</m> and <m>\cos(x)</m> on the interval <m>[0, 2\pi]</m>. Answer the following questions:
      </p>

      <sidebyside widths="56% 40%" valign="top">
        <stack>
          <p><strong>i)</strong> <m>y = \sin(x)</m></p>
          <image source="pictures/Sinx.png">
            <shortdescription>Graph of y = sin(x) over the interval [0, 2pi].</shortdescription>
          </image>
          <p><strong>ii)</strong> <m>y = \cos(x)</m></p>
          <image source="pictures/Cosx.png">
            <shortdescription>Graph of y = cos(x) over the interval [0, 2pi].</shortdescription>
          </image>
        </stack>

        <stack>
          <p workspace="0.4in"><strong>a)</strong> Where is <m>\sin(x)</m> increasing/decreasing?</p>
          <p workspace="0.4in"><strong>b)</strong> Where is <m>\cos(x)</m> positive/negative?</p>
          <p workspace="0.4in"><strong>c)</strong> Where is <m>\sin(x)</m> concave up/concave down?</p>
          <p workspace="0.4in"><strong>d)</strong> Where is <m>\cos(x)</m> increasing/decreasing?</p>
        </stack>
      </sidebyside>

      <sidebyside widths="48% 48%" valign="top">
        <p workspace="0.4in"><strong>e)</strong> Where does <m>\sin(x)</m> have horizontal tangent lines?</p>
        <p workspace="0.4in"><strong>f)</strong> Where does <m>\cos(x)</m> have roots?</p>
      </sidebyside>

      <p>
        <term>Upshot:</term> From parts a)<ndash/>f), we might guess that <m>\displaystyle \frac{d}{dx}\sin(x) =</m> <fillin characters="10"/>, but we can actually prove it algebraically.
      </p>
      <p>
        Let's use the limit definition of the derivative to do that:
        <me>\frac{d}{dx}\sin(x) = \lim_{h \to 0}\frac{\underline{\hspace{2in}}}{\underline{\hspace{2in}}}</me>
      </p>
      <p>
        Evaluating this limit relies on the following trigonometric identity and special limits:
        <me>\sin(A+B) = \sin(A)\cos(B) + \cos(A)\sin(B), \quad \lim_{x \to 0} \frac{\cos(x)-1}{x} = 0, \quad \text{and} \quad \lim_{x \to 0} \frac{\sin(x)}{x} = 1.</me>
      </p>
      <p>
        (The full algebraic proof is omitted. You do not need to know how to carry out this derivation on assessments.)
      </p>
      <p>
        So we see that indeed, <m>\displaystyle \frac{d}{dx}\sin(x) =</m> <fillin characters="10"/>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Thankfully, the other 5 trig functions are easier to differentiate.
      </p>
      <p>
        We previously know that <m>\sin(x)</m> and <m>\cos(x)</m> have a wave-like shape. By applying transformations, such as shifting by <m>\pi/2</m> and reflecting over the <m>x</m>-axis, we get the following:
        <me>\cos(x) = \sin(x+\pi/2) \quad \text{and} \quad \sin(x) = -\cos(x+\pi/2)</me>
        Using the derivative of <m>\sin(x)</m> and the Chain Rule, we obtain
        <me>\frac{d}{dx}\cos(x) = \underline{\hspace{3.5in}}.</me>
      </p>
      <p workspace="1.1in">
        Knowing the derivatives of <m>\sin(x)</m> and <m>\cos(x)</m>, we can use the Quotient Rule to find the derivative of <m>\tan(x)</m>!
      </p>
      <p>
        The remaining three trigonometric functions (<m>\cot(x)</m>, <m>\sec(x)</m>, and <m>\csc(x)</m>) can also be written in terms of <m>\sin(x)</m> and <m>\cos(x)</m>, so we can find their derivatives using similar strategies (we'll omit that work for the sake of time, but it's a GREAT thing to try to work out if you want more practice<mdash/>you can do it!).
      </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs xml:id="p-trig-patterns">
      <title>Summary and Patterns of Trigonometric Derivatives</title>
      <p>
        Here are the derivatives we expect you to know, along with patterns that will help us remember them:
      </p>

      <tabular top="minor" left="minor" right="minor" bottom="minor">
        <col width="25%" halign="center"/>
        <col width="35%" halign="center"/>
        <row bottom="minor">
          <cell><m>f(x)</m></cell>
          <cell><m>f'(x)</m></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><m>\sin(x)</m></cell>
          <cell><m>\cos(x)</m></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><m>\cos(x)</m></cell>
          <cell><m>-\sin(x)</m></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><m>\tan(x)</m></cell>
          <cell><m>\sec^2(x)</m></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><m>\sec(x)</m></cell>
          <cell><m>\sec(x)\tan(x)</m></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><m>\csc(x)</m></cell>
          <cell><m>-\csc(x)\cot(x)</m></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><m>\cot(x)</m></cell>
          <cell><m>-\csc^2(x)</m></cell>
        </row>
      </tabular>

      <sidebyside widths="22% 23% 26% 24%" margins="1% 1%" valign="top">
        <tabular top="minor" left="minor" right="minor" bottom="minor">
          <col width="45%" halign="center"/>
          <col width="55%" halign="center"/>
          <row bottom="minor">
            <cell><m>f(x)</m></cell>
            <cell><m>f'(x)</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><m>\sin(x)</m></cell>
            <cell><m>\cos(x)</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><m>\cos(x)</m></cell>
            <cell><m>-\sin(x)</m></cell>
          </row>
        </tabular>

        <tabular top="minor" left="minor" right="minor" bottom="minor">
          <col width="45%" halign="center"/>
          <col width="55%" halign="center"/>
          <row bottom="minor">
            <cell><m>f(x)</m></cell>
            <cell><m>f'(x)</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><m>\tan(x)</m></cell>
            <cell><m>\sec^2(x)</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><m>\cot(x)</m></cell>
            <cell><m>-\csc^2(x)</m></cell>
          </row>
        </tabular>

        <tabular top="minor" left="minor" right="minor" bottom="minor">
          <col width="42%" halign="center"/>
          <col width="58%" halign="center"/>
          <row bottom="minor">
            <cell><m>f(x)</m></cell>
            <cell><m>f'(x)</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><m>\cos(x)</m></cell>
            <cell><m>-\sin(x)</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><m>\csc(x)</m></cell>
            <cell><m>-\csc(x)\cot(x)</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><m>\cot(x)</m></cell>
            <cell><m>-\csc^2(x)</m></cell>
          </row>
        </tabular>

        <tabular top="minor" left="minor" right="minor" bottom="minor">
          <col width="42%" halign="center"/>
          <col width="58%" halign="center"/>
          <row bottom="minor">
            <cell><m>f(x)</m></cell>
            <cell><m>f'(x)</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><m>\sec(x)</m></cell>
            <cell><m>\sec(x)\tan(x)</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><m>\csc(x)</m></cell>
            <cell><m>-\csc(x)\cot(x)</m></cell>
          </row>
        </tabular>
      </sidebyside>

      <p>
        This wraps up almost all of our tools for finding derivatives. Next week, we'll discuss implicit differentiation, which helps us find the derivatives of logarithmic functions.
      </p>
    </paragraphs>
  </page>

  <page>
    <paragraphs xml:id="p-rates-of-change-day16">
      <title>Derivatives as Rates of Change</title>
      <p>
        Let's finish today by taking a step back and thinking about an application of derivatives. The big idea here is that derivatives model the rates at which things change.
      </p>
      <p>
        One of the real-world reasons people care about derivatives is that they help us relate tons of things to each other. For example, position, velocity, and acceleration:
        <md>
          <mrow>s(t) \amp \text{ gives an object's position at time } t</mrow>
          <mrow>s'(t) = v(t) \amp \text{ gives the object's velocity at time } t</mrow>
          <mrow>s''(t) = v'(t) = a(t) \amp \text{ gives the object's acceleration at time } t</mrow>
        </md>
      </p>
      <p>
        <term>Notes:</term> Velocity is speed with direction. That is, <m>\text{speed} = \lvert\text{velocity}\rvert</m>, and the sign of a velocity tells you which direction your object is traveling.
      </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs xml:id="prob-dynamite-blast-day16">
      <title>Problem 1</title>
      <p>
        <em>(Note: This problem may have already been discussed on Monday.)</em> A dynamite blast blows a heavy rock straight up with an initial velocity of <m>10\text{ ft/s}</m>. It reaches a height of <m>s = 10t - t^2\text{ ft}</m> after <m>t</m> seconds.
      </p>

      <sidebyside widths="24% 72%" valign="top">
        <image source="pictures/sec34fa24pic1.png">
          <shortdescription>
            Vertical trajectory diagram of a rock launched straight up from the ground, reaching a maximum height before falling back down.
          </shortdescription>
        </image>
        <stack>
          <p workspace="1.3in">
            <strong>a)</strong> What are the velocity and acceleration of the rock at any time <m>t</m> during its flight (after the blast)?
          </p>
          <p workspace="1.3in">
            <strong>b)</strong> When does the rock reach its highest point, and how high does it go?
          </p>
        </stack>
      </sidebyside>

      <p workspace="1.5in">
        <strong>c)</strong> What are the velocity and speed of the rock when it is 16 ft above the ground on the way up? On the way down?
      </p>
      <p workspace="1.5in">
        <strong>d)</strong> When does the rock hit the ground again?
      </p>
    </paragraphs>
  </page>

  <page>
    <paragraphs xml:id="prob-bacterial-population">
      <title>Problem 2</title>
      <p>
        When extra nutrients were added to a broth in which bacteria were growing, the size of the population at time <m>t</m> (in hours) was
        <me>p(t) = 10^6 + 10^4t - 10^3\sin(t\pi).</me>
      </p>
      <p workspace="2.2in">
        <strong>a)</strong> Find the rates of change of the population at <m>t = 0</m> hours, <m>\displaystyle t = \frac{1}{2}</m> hour, and <m>t = 1</m> hour.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>b)</strong> Based on your results from part (a), which of the following best describes the bacterial population during the first hour (<m>0 \leq t \leq 1</m>)?
      </p>
      <ol marker="(A)">
        <li><p>The population is increasing, and the growth rate is increasing.</p></li>
        <li><p>The population is increasing, but the growth rate is decreasing.</p></li>
        <li><p>The population is decreasing, and the growth rate is increasing.</p></li>
        <li><p>The population is decreasing, but the growth rate is decreasing.</p></li>
      </ol>
    </paragraphs>
  </page>
</handout>